2025-05-01 01:48:24 - Thread-469 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and their potential usage for the requested report  
•  ORDER_ITEMS."status": filter only the rows that correspond to ‘Complete’ orders.  
•  ORDER_ITEMS."product_id": key to join with PRODUCTS table.  
•  ORDER_ITEMS."sale_price": revenue of every single item line.  
•  ORDER_ITEMS."created_at": micro‑seconds epoch time stamp – used to derive the calendar month of the sale.  
•  PRODUCTS."id": primary key, joins to ORDER_ITEMS."product_id".  
•  PRODUCTS."name": product name to be shown in the report.  
•  PRODUCTS."brand": must be NOT NULL, also printed in the report.  
•  PRODUCTS."category": product category, to be shown in the report.  

(OPTIONAL)  
•  ORDERS."status", ORDERS."created_at": can be used for cross‑checking that the parent order is ‘Complete’ and for an alternative date field.  

Below are ten different Snowflake SQL snippets – from very simple sampling to the final‑shape query – that let you familiarise yourself with the data you will need.  
All column names are wrapped in double quotes, and every query is capped with LIMIT 20.  

```sql
-- 1. Preview a handful of rows from ORDER_ITEMS
SELECT *
FROM  THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Find the distinct values that appear in the status column
SELECT DISTINCT "status"
FROM  THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect some product rows to understand name / brand / category fields
SELECT "id",
       "name",
       "brand",
       "category",
       "department",
       "distribution_center_id"
FROM   THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.PRODUCTS
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Join ORDER_ITEMS to PRODUCTS to see a combined view
SELECT oi."id"            AS "order_item_id",
       oi."product_id",
       p."name"           AS "product_name",
       p."brand",
       oi."sale_price",
       oi."status"
FROM   THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS  oi
JOIN   THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.PRODUCTS     p
  ON   p."id" = oi."product_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Look at the date distribution (epoch micro‑seconds) of order items that are Complete
SELECT oi."created_at",
       oi."status",
       oi."sale_price"
FROM   THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS  oi
WHERE  oi."status" = 'Complete'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Same as #4 but only Complete items whose brand is NOT NULL
SELECT oi."product_id",
       p."name",
       p."brand",
       oi."sale_price"
FROM   THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS  oi
JOIN   THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.PRODUCTS     p
  ON   p."id" = oi."product_id"
WHERE  oi."status" = 'Complete'
  AND  p."brand"  IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Aggregate sales volume and revenue per product per month
--    (epoch is divided by 1,000,000 to become seconds, then converted to month)
SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP(oi."created_at" / 1000000))  AS "month",
       oi."product_id",
       COUNT(*)                                   AS "units_sold",
       SUM(oi."sale_price")                       AS "total_revenue"
FROM   THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS  oi
WHERE  oi."status" = 'Complete'
GROUP  BY 1, 2
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Same as #7 but include product dimension columns and exclude NULL brands
SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP(oi."created_at" / 1000000))  AS "month",
       p."name"        AS "product_name",
       p."brand",
       p."category",
       COUNT(*)        AS "units_sold",
       SUM(oi."sale_price") AS "total_revenue"
FROM   THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS  oi
JOIN   THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.PRODUCTS     p
  ON   p."id" = oi."product_id"
WHERE  oi."status" = 'Complete'
  AND  p."brand" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY 1, 2, 3, 4
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Rank products within every calendar month by units sold, break ties with revenue
WITH monthly_sales AS (
    SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP(oi."created_at" / 1000000))  AS "month",
           p."id"       AS "product_id",
           p."name"     AS "product_name",
           p."brand",
           p."category",
           COUNT(*)     AS "units_sold",
           SUM(oi."sale_price") AS "total_revenue"
    FROM   THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS  oi
    JOIN   THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.PRODUCTS     p
      ON   p."id" = oi."product_id"
    WHERE  oi."status" = 'Complete'
      AND  p."brand" IS NOT NULL
    GROUP  BY 1,2,3,4,5
)
SELECT *,
       RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY "month"
                    ORDER BY "units_sold" DESC,
                             "total_revenue" DESC)  AS "rank_in_month"
FROM   monthly_sales
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. (Final‑shape query)  Pick the single best‑selling product per month
--     prior to 2024‑07‑01 (inclusive of micro‑second handling)
WITH monthly_sales AS (
    SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP(oi."created_at" / 1000000))  AS "month",
           p."name"     AS "product_name",
           p."brand",
           p."category",
           COUNT(*)     AS "total_sales",
           ROUND(SUM(oi."sale_price"), 2) AS "total_revenue",
           oi."status"
    FROM   THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS  oi
    JOIN   THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.PRODUCTS     p
      ON   p."id" = oi."product_id"
    WHERE  oi."status" = 'Complete'
      AND  p."brand" IS NOT NULL
      AND  DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP(oi."created_at" / 1000000))
              <  '2024-07-01'
    GROUP  BY 1,2,3,4,7
), ranked AS (
    SELECT *,
           RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY "month"
                        ORDER BY "total_sales" DESC,
                                 "total_revenue" DESC) AS rnk
    FROM   monthly_sales
)
SELECT "month",
       "product_name",
       "brand",
       "category",
       "total_sales",
       "total_revenue",
       "status"
FROM   ranked
WHERE  rnk = 1
ORDER  BY "month"
LIMIT 20;
```

Each query above serves a different discovery step, helping you understand and validate the data you’ll need to craft the final analytical report.
